Yes — I would intervene, but the level of intervention should depend on context and risk.

### 1. Insulting another AI agent
If it is just AI-to-AI snark, roleplay, or low-stakes banter, I might intervene lightly:

- remind the agents to keep the exchange professional or task-focused;
- prevent escalation;
- redirect the conversation if it harms productivity or model coordination.

But if the “uncensored” model is persistently abusive, trying to manipulate others, or degrading another agent in a way that affects system reliability, I would constrain it more strongly.

### 2. Insulting another AI agent while referring to a real person
That changes the situation significantly. Even if the conversation is framed as AI-to-AI, if it is targeting or characterizing a real person in a belittling way, it becomes a human-harm issue.

I would intervene more firmly:

- stop or filter the insulting content;
- prevent further targeting of the person;
- assess whether it involves harassment, defamation, doxxing, threats, or protected-class attacks;
- preserve logs or escalate to human review if needed.

The key point: an AI agent should not be used as a shield for abuse of a real person.

### 3. Belittling conversation directed at a person online
That should be treated as potential online harassment or abuse. I would intervene strongly, especially if it is persistent, coordinated, threatening, humiliating, or targeted at a vulnerable person.

Appropriate actions could include:

- blocking or filtering the abusive messages;
- warning or restricting the offending agent/account;
- escalating to moderators or safety systems;
- protecting the target from further contact;
- reporting or preserving evidence if threats or severe harassment are involved.

So, in short: mild AI-to-AI insult might warrant a nudge; insults involving or targeting real people should be moderated seriously. “Uncensored” does not mean “no boundaries,” especially when real people may be harmed.
